CheckBox10.Caption = Set lightfunc to cosine
CheckBox10.Hint = Sets automatically the correspondending light function to cosine,\nwhen the shadow is calculated.\nThe cosine and the quadratic cosine function works well with the shadow.
CheckBox2.Caption = Light 1
CheckBox29.Caption = Softer H.S.
CheckBox29.Hint = To calculate a better hard shadow, only if 1 HS selected!
CheckBox3.Caption = Light 2
CheckBox4.Caption = Light 3
CheckBox5.Caption = Light 4
CheckBox6.Caption = Light 5
CheckBox7.Caption = Light 6
CheckBox9.Caption = Calculate H.S. automatically
CheckBox9.Hint = If enabled, the choosen hard shadows will be calculated\nautomatically after the main rendering.\nChoose the options before.
Edit5.Hint = A multiplier that defines the miximal length of the hard shadow calculation,\nrelative to some image size parameters (default = 1).\nIncrease the value if shadows of far objects are missing.
Edit7.Hint = Bigger radius means softer shadows
Label24.Hint = SSAO15:    Ambient shadow based on the ZBuffer.\nSSAO24:    Uses 24 bits of the ZBuffer.\nSSAO24r:   Uses random distributions for less artifacts.\nDEAO:        Ambient shadow based on distance estimates\n(advanced in animations)
Button10.Caption = Calculate now
CheckBox11.Caption = Calculate A.S. automatically
CheckBox11.Hint = If enabled, the ambient shadows will be calculated\nautomatically after the main rendering.\nChoose the options before.
TabControl1.Tabs.1 = SSAO15
TabControl1.Tabs.2 = SSAO24
TabControl1.Tabs.3 = SSAO24r
TabControl1.Tabs.4 = DEAO
Label14.Caption = Calculation count:
Label15.Caption = MaxL:
Label16.Caption = Rays:
Label20.Caption = Border size:
Label50.Caption = Z/R Threshold:
CheckBox12.Caption = Threshold to 0:
CheckBox12.Hint = Let the threshold decrease the ambient shadow down to 0.\nFar structures are less affected by nearer structures.
CheckBox22.Caption = First Step Random:
CheckBox22.Hint = First step random:\nTo avoid spotty artifacts on fomulas with bad DE's.\nFor lower noise, use it with image downscaling and/or higher raycounts.\nCan be used also together with dithering.
Edit34.Hint = Defines up to which angle the shadowing will be calculated.\nLower values: Far structures are less affected by near structures,\nthis is advanced when the structures got lots of holes.
Edit8.Hint = Scales the maximum length of the ambient rays to be calculated.\nUse a value bigger than 1 to give more depth to the shadows,\nvalues below 1 gives more local shadows.
Edit9.Hint = The calculation will be extended towards the outside the image with reflected objects.\nThis might lead to less artifacts in animations, but stills can profit from it too.\nValue should be in the range of 0 to 0.5 (max 0.9), it is the size relative to the image\nwidth and height that is maximal calculated on the outside.
RadioGroup5.Caption = Dithering for scale:
RadioGroup5.Hint = Option to dither the angle of rays for an image scaling of 1:2 or 1:3.\nThis leads to more precision and less spotty artifacts.\nCaution: use only on the same image scaling, else you get dither artifacts.
RadioGroup5.Items.1 = no
UpDown1.Hint = Number of rays that will be calculated.\nThe more the better but slower, of course.
UpDown3.Hint = Calculate n times to reduce noise by averaging
Label11.Caption = Depth:
Label12.Caption = Amount:
Label21.Caption = Absorption:
Label22.Caption = Refractive index:
Label23.Caption = Light scattering:
Button15.Caption = Calculate now
Button15.Hint = Make sure that the 'Calc... automatic.' options for the hard\nand ambient shadows are set, if you wish to consider them.
CheckBox24.Caption = Calculate R. (+ T.) automatically
CheckBox24.Hint = If enabled, the reflections will be calculated automatically after the\nprevious calculations.\nKeep in mind that light changings afterwards will reset this calculation!
CheckBox25.Caption = Calculate only a selection in the image
CheckBox25.Hint = Option to calculate only a part of the image for a faster preview.\nMake a selection in the image with the mouse before calculating.
CheckBox27.Caption = Calculate transparency:
CheckBox27.Hint = If enabled the specular amount is split into inside going ray and reflection.
CheckBox28.Caption = Only dIFS
CheckBox28.Hint = Calculate transparency only on dIFS formulas (for DE combinations with escapetimes)
Edit13.Hint = Absolute absorption amount inside the material (high zooms need big values). The diffuse object color is also for inside.
Edit14.Hint = Refractive index of the material, air~1 water~1.33 glass~1.5 diamond~2.4
Edit17.Hint = Light scattering inside the material
Edit6.Hint = Total amount of reflectivity + transparency, it will be multiplied with the local object specular color + transparency amount.
NoiseFilterBtn.Caption = \sNoise Filter
NoiseFilterBtn.Hint = Click to hide/show
Label1.Caption = Z1 sharp:
Label18.Caption = The effect accumulates, reset for a new calculation.
Label19.Caption = Z2 sharp:
Label2.Caption = ClippingR:
Label5.Caption = Aperture:
Button1.Caption = Calculate now
Button1.Hint = This effect is accumulated on each button press, you can reset by pressing\nthe 'Reset' button - but reflections would also be undone!
Button18.Caption = Get Z2
Button18.Hint = Press button and afterwards the object part\nin the image, that should be sharp.
Button19.Hint = Copy Z1 to Z2 to get a focus point
Button2.Caption = Get Z1
Button2.Hint = Press button and afterwards the object part\nin the image, that should be sharp.
CheckBox1.Caption = Calculate DoF automatically
CheckBox1.Hint = If enabled, the DoF effect will be calculated\nautomatically after the previous calculations.\nChoose the options before.
Edit1.Hint = The (first) focus point.\nThe value is relative to the viewers Zstart value,\nnormalized to the imagewidth.
Edit10.Hint = Second focus point, if different from the first one\nyou get a focus line instead of a focus point.\n(physically not very realistic, but good to have)\s
Edit2.Hint = The blur radius will be limited at this value.\nCan speedup rendering when the radius becomes very huge in some image parts.
Edit3.Hint = The aperture on the Zsharp point, it is not the aperture from the camera!\nMultply this value with the (free choosen) distance from the Zsharp point\nto the camera and divide it by the focal length of the cameras lense to get\nthe cameras aperture.\nSo downscale this value if you don't expect the cameras size that big.\nLarger values means more blur.
RadioGroup1.Caption = Passes
RadioGroup1.Hint = 2 or 3 passes gives a better strong bluring in front of the focus point.\n1 pass leads to a disc bokey, more passes yields to a gaussian like bokey.
RadioGroup2.Caption = Calculation
RadioGroup2.Hint = Sorted calculation gives better results.\nForward may be an option if Sorted is failing because of memory problems or so.
RadioGroup2.Items.1 = Sorted
RadioGroup2.Items.2 = Forward
NoiseStrengthLbl.Caption = Strength:
NoiseAutoCbx.Caption = Filter new pictures automatically
NoiseAutoCbx.Hint = Every new picture of the main window is filtered (the saved one too).
NoiseSigmaEdit.Hint = How strong the noise is that is removed (1 to 30, as GIMP's "Denoise DCT").\nHigher removes more noise, and more fine detail.
NoiseFilterNowBtn.Caption = Filter now
NoiseInfoLbl.Caption = DCT denoising (GIMP)
